Dàn bài:

```markdown
# Writing a TurboModule from scratch — and then doing it the easy way

## Why I did this
Not because the app needed it. Because I wanted to know what the Expo Modules
API hides, and "I understand the New Architecture conceptually" is a weaker
sentence than "I've written one."

## The spec is the contract
[TypeScript spec, why the filename must start with Native, what Codegen produces]

## Android: Kotlin
[Extending the generated class, sync vs async, why needsEagerInit matters]

## iOS: the extra layer
[Why Swift can't subclass C++, the .mm bridge, the Swift header trap]

## Proving lazy loading
[The constructor log experiment — the actual output]

## Then the easy way
[Expo Modules API, ~60 lines vs ~180, no Objective-C++]

## What I'd pick, and when
[The decision table]

## What surprised me
[Be honest. What took longest? What did you get wrong first?]
```

**Phần cuối là phần đắt nhất.** Người đọc tin một bài viết có kể chỗ tác giả sai. Bài nào cũng trơn tru từ đầu tới cuối thì đọc như quảng cáo.

### 14:45–15:00 · Cập nhật README

```markdown
## Native module

The app ships a small native module that reads device model, battery level and
low-power state. When the battery drops below 20% or the OS enters low-power
mode, background refetching is paused and transitions are disabled.

I implemented it twice — once as a raw TurboModule (TypeScript spec → Codegen →
Kotlin on Android, Swift behind an Objective-C++ bridge on iOS) and once with
the Expo Modules API. Write-up: [docs/writing-a-turbomodule.md](docs/writing-a-turbomodule.md)
```

